WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.932
Picture a city library on the night of its yearly count.

2
00:00:05.932 --> 00:00:10.318
By morning, the council wants to know how many books it owns by each author.

3
00:00:10.318 --> 00:00:13.088
And the books sit in forty branches across town.

4
00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:17.872
The obvious plan: bring every book to the central library and count them there.

5
00:00:17.872 --> 00:00:23.584
With forty branches, that's a building too small for the books, and a count still going next week.

6
00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.592
The better plan sends the counting to the books.

7
00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:30.948
Each branch counts its own shelves, and sends on short notes, never books.

8
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:33.078
Computers have the same problem.

9
00:00:33.078 --> 00:00:38.060
A payment company keeps a log of payment events, and it's too big for one machine.

10
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.219
So it's cut into blocks, spread over three machines.

11
00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:43.832
Each machine in the group is called a node.

12
00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:47.586
The question: how many times does each status appear?

13
00:00:47.586 --> 00:00:49.529
Approved, declined, refunded.

14
00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:51.872
Say three machines do the counting.

15
00:00:51.872 --> 00:00:54.708
The word approved appears on all three nodes.

16
00:00:54.708 --> 00:00:57.858
How many of the counters end up counting approved?

17
00:00:57.858 --> 00:00:58.867
Pause and guess.

18
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:03.596
Hold on to your guess.

19
00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.195
Let's build the answer.

20
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.430
Version one: every node sends every status it reads to one counting machine.

21
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.283
For nine words, that's fine.

22
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:17.630
Now ask a bigger question: how many payments per card, over a billion cards.

23
00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:22.114
The counter now receives the whole input through one network link.

24
00:01:22.114 --> 00:01:27.889
One terabyte, through a link that moves 125 megabytes a second, takes over two hours.

25
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:33.539
And it has to keep a billion counters in memory, on a machine with two to four gigabytes.

26
00:01:33.539 --> 00:01:35.568
That's the central library again.

27
00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:39.328
What we want is many counters, each with a share of the work.

28
00:01:39.328 --> 00:01:43.823
But then every node must know where to send each note, without asking anyone.

29
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:45.961
Let's try the easy rule first.

30
00:01:45.961 --> 00:01:48.887
Send each note to whichever counter is free.

31
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.105
Watch approved.

32
00:01:50.105 --> 00:01:52.584
Its notes land on all three counters.

33
00:01:52.584 --> 00:01:56.404
Each one has part of the count, and nobody has the total.

34
00:01:56.404 --> 00:01:59.687
So now we need one more step to add the parts up.

35
00:01:59.687 --> 00:02:00.156
Well...

36
00:02:00.156 --> 00:02:03.775
we just built the central library again, only smaller.

37
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:05.162
Back to the library.

38
00:02:05.162 --> 00:02:07.681
There, every author has one owner branch.

39
00:02:07.681 --> 00:02:14.684
Every branch sends its notes for that author to the owner, and works out the owner by itself, from the name alone.

40
00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:15.851
Let's map that.

41
00:02:15.851 --> 00:02:17.624
The branches are the nodes.

42
00:02:17.624 --> 00:02:19.462
The books are the log lines.

43
00:02:19.462 --> 00:02:22.417
A note is a pair: a word, and the number one.

44
00:02:22.417 --> 00:02:25.240
The word is the key, whatever you group by.

45
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.472
And the owner branch is a counter.

46
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.605
The rule that picks the owner is a hash function.

47
00:02:30.605 --> 00:02:36.616
It turns a value into a number, and the same value always gives the same number, on every machine.

48
00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:40.444
Divide that number by three and keep the remainder.

49
00:02:40.444 --> 00:02:42.851
That's a counter from zero to two.

50
00:02:42.851 --> 00:02:44.196
Approved gives one.

51
00:02:44.196 --> 00:02:45.612
Declined gives zero.

52
00:02:45.612 --> 00:02:46.957
Refunded gives two.

53
00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:51.392
Hadoop, the free copy of Google's design, writes the rule like this.

54
00:02:51.392 --> 00:02:58.155
A hash code can be negative, so the first part clears the sign, to keep the remainder a real counter number.

55
00:02:58.333 --> 00:02:59.117
Now run it.

56
00:02:59.117 --> 00:03:04.030
Map: each node reads its own block, and turns every word into a pair.

57
00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.311
Nine pairs in all.

58
00:03:05.311 --> 00:03:07.875
No node knows what the others found.

59
00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:12.708
Each node tags every pair with its owner, and writes them to its own disk.

60
00:03:12.708 --> 00:03:15.108
Approved is tagged one, on every node.

61
00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:20.164
The shuffle: each counter fetches its pairs from every node, over the network.

62
00:03:20.164 --> 00:03:23.555
Then it sorts them by key, so equal keys sit together.

63
00:03:23.555 --> 00:03:27.511
With millions of keys, that lets it finish one key and move on.

64
00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:33.012
Reduce: each counter adds up each key, and writes one output file.

65
00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.139
Declined, two.

66
00:03:34.139 --> 00:03:35.346
Approved, five.

67
00:03:35.346 --> 00:03:36.473
Refunded, two.

68
00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:37.658
So, your guess?

69
00:03:37.658 --> 00:03:40.116
Exactly one counter counts approved.

70
00:03:40.116 --> 00:03:44.760
No last step adds anything up, because every key lives in one place.

71
00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:50.525
This is MapReduce, published by Jeff Dean and Sanjay Ghemawat of Google in 2004.

72
00:03:50.525 --> 00:03:52.610
You write two small functions.

73
00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:54.833
Map turns one record into pairs.

74
00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:58.585
Reduce turns one key and all its values into a result.

75
00:03:58.585 --> 00:04:01.295
Everything between them is the shuffle.

76
00:04:01.295 --> 00:04:03.865
And the counters are called reducers.

77
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.391
Now let's lose a machine on purpose.

78
00:04:06.391 --> 00:04:11.437
Node B finishes its map, and then dies, before the reducers fetch its pairs.

79
00:04:11.633 --> 00:04:13.713
Its map task already finished.

80
00:04:13.713 --> 00:04:15.515
Does it have to run again?

81
00:04:15.515 --> 00:04:16.624
Pause and guess.

82
00:04:19.867 --> 00:04:20.143
Yes.

83
00:04:20.143 --> 00:04:23.869
Those pairs sat on B's own disk, so they died with it.

84
00:04:23.869 --> 00:04:31.599
The master, the program that hands out the tasks, runs map two again on node C, which keeps a copy of block two.

85
00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:36.368
The reducers fetch from C instead, and the totals come out the same.

86
00:04:36.368 --> 00:04:41.849
That only works because map is deterministic: same input, same pairs, every time.

87
00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:44.177
Finished reducers never run again.

88
00:04:44.177 --> 00:04:47.960
Their files are already in the file system, in three copies.

89
00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:52.059
In the library, a lost note is just written again from the books.

90
00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:54.897
How much does a failure cost?

91
00:04:54.897 --> 00:05:03.513
In the paper's one terabyte sort, the authors killed 200 of the 1,746 workers a few minutes in.

92
00:05:03.513 --> 00:05:07.141
The job took 933 seconds instead of 891.

93
00:05:07.141 --> 00:05:08.955
Five percent slower.

94
00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:10.494
Two things to watch.

95
00:05:10.494 --> 00:05:13.556
Change the number of reducers, and keys move.

96
00:05:13.556 --> 00:05:17.025
With four, approved goes to reducer three, not one.

97
00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:18.858
And one key never splits.

98
00:05:18.858 --> 00:05:25.225
If most of the log is approved, one reducer gets most of the work, and the whole job waits for it.

99
00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.007
So, the moral: count where the data sits.

100
00:05:28.007 --> 00:05:32.777
Send each key to one owner, picked by a rule every machine works out alone.

101
00:05:32.777 --> 00:05:35.703
And if a machine dies, just do its work again.

102
00:05:36.167 --> 00:05:37.289
One quick check.

103
00:05:37.289 --> 00:05:39.675
Declined appears on nodes A and C.

104
00:05:39.675 --> 00:05:42.623
How many reducers count it, and which one?

105
00:05:42.623 --> 00:05:43.745
Pause and think.

106
00:05:46.933 --> 00:05:47.350
One.

107
00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:49.955
Reducer zero, every time.
